speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, the main purpose of this bill is made clear in the fact that it will repeal the Diplomatic Immunities (Commonwealth Countries) Act and replace it by the Diplomatic and Consular Privileges and Immunities Act. In other words, it proposes to broaden things to deal not only with diplomats but with consular representatives, and that applies both ways. There is also the question of giving a certain status to the two Vienna Conventions. This is a step we should be taking.
The bill, with its schedules, is a complicated one which should be studied carefully in the Standing Committee on External Affairs and National Defence. The smart thing to do this afternoon is to send the bill to that committee. I am sure the points raised by the last speaker, particularly with respect to the abuse of immunities, will be dealt with in that committee. The members, generally, will go into this bill and all its provisions very carefully. We are happy to see the bill get second reading and be sent to committee this afternoon.
[Translation}
